REAL run against real Auth/Storage/PostgREST over HTTP, not a SQL-only helper
check — every persona signed in via supabase.auth.signInWithPassword and every
read/sign/update/delete went through the actual Storage REST API.

An earlier attempt (kept as a documented finding, not hidden) showed
groomerUnassigned/evidenceAdminB/financeOnly all incorrectly ALLOWED to read
Andi's grooming evidence. Root-caused via direct SQL impersonation
(set_config('request.jwt.claims', ...) + calling app.fn_attachments_object_readable
directly) to a TEST FIXTURE bug, not a policy bug: the attachments metadata row
for the evidence object had not yet been (re-)applied via
s1_storage_fixtures.sql, so every read fell through to the function's
deliberate "nothing committed at this path yet" allowance (the same narrow
mid-upload-race fallback that lets a fresh uploader read their own
in-flight object before its attachments row exists). Once the fixture was
applied, the same real HTTP test correctly denied all three personas — see
the 23/23 run above. The destructive nature of the delete-evidence test
means this script is single-shot per `supabase db reset` +
s1_storage_fixtures.sql application: rerunning it against already-consumed
fixtures (the evidence attachment soft-deleted, its bytes removed) will
show the same class of false "Object not found" outcomes for an unrelated
reason (fixture already consumed, not a policy gap) — reset and reapply
fixtures before rerunning.

